  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Ocellated Emerald is a small to medium-sized dragonfly belonging to the family Corduliidae, characterized by its brilliant green coloration and distinctive ocelli (eye-like spots). This species is commonly found throughout New England where it hunts insects near freshwater habitats during the summer months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ocelli Pattern",
      "description": "Look for distinctive dark spots or ocelli on the abdomen that resemble eyes, which give this species its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Metallic Green Body",
      "description": "The head, thorax, and abdomen display a vibrant metallic green coloration that shimmers in sunlight",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Adults typically measure 1.3 to 1.6 inches in length, making them one of the smaller emerald dragonfly species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "The Ocellated Emerald inhabits areas around clear, cool streams and small ponds with abundant vegetation in New England's forests and wetlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many dragonfly species, Ocellated Emeralds are relatively weak fliers and tend to remain close to their breeding waters, making them excellent indicators of healthy freshwater ecosystems.",
